galfus-animation:
---
name: galfus-animation
description: >-
  Use this skill to configure keyframe and skeletal animations in Galfus Engine,
  including AnimationClip, AnimationPlayer3D, crossfading, loop modes, and pose blending.
---

# Galfus Engine Animation System

Galfus Engine provides a hardware-accelerated skeletal and keyframe animation system. It supports multi-track sampling, cubic spline/linear interpolation, crossfade transitions, and skeletal skinning.

## 1. Core Concepts

- **KeyframeTrack**: Samples translation (`vec3`), rotation (`quat`), scale (`vec3`), or morph weights over time.
- **AnimationClip**: A named collection of tracks with a total duration.
- **AnimationPlayer3DComponent**: ECS component attached to an entity to coordinate playback, weights, and crossfading.
- **createAnimationSystem3D**: The ECS system that evaluates active player clips and applies transformations every tick.

## 2. Setting Up the Animation System

Attach the animation evaluation system to your realm's ECS world:

```typescript
import { createAnimationSystem3D, type AnimationPlayer3DComponent } from '@galfus/engine';

// Register the animation system with the realm's ECS world
const animSystem = createAnimationSystem3D(realm3d.world, realm3d);
realm3d.world.addSystem(animSystem);
```

## 3. Creating & Attaching an Animation Player

```typescript
import { attachAnimationPlayer3D } from '@galfus/engine';

// Attach player component to a character root entity
const player = attachAnimationPlayer3D(realm3d.world, characterEntity);

// Register animation clips (e.g. from glTF loader or procedurally created)
player.addClip(walkClip);
player.addClip(runClip);
player.addClip(idleClip);
```

## 4. Playback Controls & Loop Modes

Supported loop modes: `'repeat'` (infinite loop), `'once'` (plays once and stops), `'ping-pong'` (reverses at boundaries).

```typescript
// 1. Play idle animation looped at normal speed
player.play('Idle', 'repeat', 1.0);

// 2. Play attack animation once at 1.5x speed
player.play('Attack', 'once', 1.5);

// 3. Stop specific clip or all playing clips
player.stop('Walk');
// player.stop(); // Stops all
```

## 5. Smooth Crossfading Between Clips

Smoothly interpolate between two animation states (e.g. from Idle to Run) over a specified duration in seconds:

```typescript
// Crossfade from current active clip to 'Run' over 0.35 seconds
player.crossfade('Run', 0.35, 'repeat');
```

## 6. Procedural Keyframe Tracks

You can also construct `AnimationClip` instances manually in code:

```typescript
import type { AnimationClip, KeyframeTrack } from '@galfus/engine';

const bounceTrack: KeyframeTrack = {
  targetEntity: cube.entityId,
  path: 'translation',
  interpolation: 'linear',
  timestamps: new Float32Array([0.0, 0.5, 1.0]),
  values: new Float32Array([
    0,
    0,
    0, // t = 0.0s: position [0, 0, 0]
    0,
    2,
    0, // t = 0.5s: position [0, 2, 0]
    0,
    0,
    0 // t = 1.0s: position [0, 0, 0]
  ])
};

const bounceClip: AnimationClip = {
  name: 'Bounce',
  duration: 1.0,
  tracks: [bounceTrack]
};

player.addClip(bounceClip);
player.play('Bounce', 'repeat');
```

## Best Practices

1. **ECS System Order**: Ensure `createAnimationSystem3D` runs before render synchronization in your tick pipeline.
2. **glTF Automatic Binding**: When using `@galfus/gltf-loader`, clips and skinning matrices are pre-configured automatically on instantiated models.
3. **Crossfade Hygiene**: Avoid triggering rapid, overlapping crossfades in the same frame; wait for transitions to complete or use discrete state machines.

galfus-core:
---
name: galfus-core
description: >-
  Use this skill to configure the Galfus Engine runtime lifecycle, multiverse presentation graph,
  render targets, spatial audio manager, user input events, and the ECS (entities, components, queries, systems).
---

# Galfus Engine Core Architecture & Lifecycle

Galfus Engine separates game state from presentation through a decoupled **Multiverse** architecture. The host application drives game logic and orchestrates renders via the high-level `Runtime` interface and its built-in **Entity Component System (ECS)**.

## 1. Runtime Initialization

The `Runtime` coordinates the tick loop, ECS worlds, platform windows, and audio playback. It requires a transport backend (`@galfus/transport-bun`, `@galfus/transport-node`, or `@galfus/transport-browser`).

```typescript
import { createRuntime } from '@galfus/engine';
import { createBunTransport } from '@galfus/transport-bun';

// 1. Initialize transport and runtime
const transport = createBunTransport();
const runtime = createRuntime(transport);

// 2. Start tick loop
runtime.start();
```

## 2. Multiverse Presentation Graph

A **Realm** (`Realm3D` or `Realm2D`) represents an isolated simulation space containing entities, lights, and cameras.
A **Presentation Target** represents a surface to draw onto (a desktop window or HTML5 canvas).
An **Invocation** links a Realm to a Target through a specific camera slot.

```typescript
// 1. Create a 3D realm
const realm3d = runtime.createRealm3D();

// 2. Create a desktop window target
const windowId = runtime.platform.window.create({
  title: 'Galfus Game',
  size: [1280, 720],
  resizable: true
});

const targetId = runtime.multiverse.createTarget({
  kind: { type: 'window', windowId }
});

// 3. Connect Realm to Target via Invocation (camera slot 1)
runtime.multiverse.createInvocation({
  realmId: realm3d.realmId,
  targetId,
  cameraSlot: 1,
  clearColor: [0.08, 0.1, 0.15, 1.0]
});
```

## 3. Entity Component System (ECS)

Every Realm exposes its own `EcsWorld` via `realm.world`. The ECS manages entities, components, and game logic systems across a structured 4-stage pipeline.

### Entities & Custom Components

Components are plain JavaScript objects or classes implementing `{ readonly type: string }`:

```typescript
import type { Component, EntityId } from '@galfus/engine';

// Define a custom gameplay component
interface HealthComponent extends Component {
  readonly type: 'Health';
  current: number;
  max: number;
}

// Spawn entity and attach components
const world = realm3d.world;
const player: EntityId = world.createEntity();

world.attachComponent(player, {
  type: 'Health',
  current: 100,
  max: 100
} as HealthComponent);

// Retrieve or query components
const health = world.getComponent<HealthComponent>(player, 'Health');
if (health) {
  health.current -= 10;
}

// Check entity status or destroy
if (world.isAlive(player)) {
  console.log('Player is alive');
}
// world.destroyEntity(player);
```

### Querying Entities

Query entities matching one or multiple component types:

```typescript
// Find all entities that have both 'Health' and 'Model3D'
const damageableEntities = world.query(['Health', 'Model3D']);

for (const entityId of damageableEntities) {
  const hp = world.getComponent<HealthComponent>(entityId, 'Health')!;
  // Process entity
}
```

### Creating Systems in the 4-Stage Pipeline

Galfus executes systems in 4 ordered stages every frame:

1. `'input'`: Processes incoming controller, keyboard, and pointer inputs.
2. `'update'`: Primary gameplay logic, AI, physics, and state mutations.
3. `'preRender'`: Spatial hierarchy updates, anchor resolution, and camera synchronization.
4. `'postRender'`: Frame telemetry, cleanups, and diagnostics.

```typescript
import type { SystemContext } from '@galfus/engine';

// Define an update system
function healthRegenSystem(world: any, context: SystemContext) {
  const { dt, time } = context;
  const entities = world.query(['Health']);

  for (const entityId of entities) {
    const hp = world.getComponent<HealthComponent>(entityId, 'Health')!;
    if (hp.current < hp.max) {
      hp.current = Math.min(hp.max, hp.current + 5 * dt);
    }
  }
}

// Register system to the world's pipeline
world.addSystem({
  step: 'update',
  fn: healthRegenSystem,
  priority: 10 // Higher priority runs earlier in the stage (default 0)
});
```

## 4. Game Tick & Event Loop

You can also hook directly into the global runtime tick loop:

```typescript
runtime.on('tick', ({ dt, time }) => {
  // Global frame tick (runs after ECS update steps)
});

// Stop or pause the runtime loop
// runtime.stop();
```

## 5. Input Handling (Keyboard, Pointer, Window)

Galfus unifies cross-platform inputs across Bun, Node, and Browsers:

```typescript
// Keyboard events
runtime.on('keyboard', (event) => {
  if (event.state === 'pressed') {
    if (event.logicalKey === 'Escape') {
      runtime.stop();
    }
    if (event.physicalKey === 'KeyW') {
      // Move forward
    }
  }
});

// Pointer / Mouse events
runtime.on('pointer', (event) => {
  if (event.type === 'move') {
    const [x, y] = event.position;
  }
  if (event.type === 'down' && event.button === 0) {
    // Primary click
  }
});

// Window resize events
runtime.on('window', (event) => {
  if (event.type === 'resize') {
    console.log(`Resized to ${event.size[0]}x${event.size[1]}`);
  }
});
```

## 6. Audio Management

The built-in `AudioManager` handles stereo sound effects, looping background music, and 3D spatial attenuation:

```typescript
// Load sound asset (from file path, URL, or buffer)
const sfxBuffer = await runtime.audio.loadSound('assets/sfx/explosion.wav');

// Play one-shot or looping audio
const soundId = runtime.audio.playSound(sfxBuffer, {
  volume: 0.8,
  loop: false,
  pitch: 1.0
});

// Spatial audio: update listener and emitter positions
runtime.audio.setListenerPosition([0, 1.7, 0]);
runtime.audio.setEmitterPosition(soundId, [10, 0, 5]);
```

## Best Practices & Gotchas

1. **Logical vs Physical IDs**: Never invent internal IDs. Let `runtime.multiverse`, `realm.createCamera`, and `runtime.platform` return managed logical IDs.
2. **Camera Slots**: Invocations bind to a `cameraSlot` integer (e.g., `1`). Ensure the camera in your realm matches the invocation's `cameraSlot`.
3. **Spatial Hierarchy Requirement**: Components like `Mesh3D`/`Collider3D` require `Model3D` on the entity; `Sprite2D`/`Collider2D` require `Model2D`.
4. **Graceful Shutdown**: Call `runtime.stop()` to clean up background worker threads and native GPU resources before process exit.

galfus-physics-2d:
---
name: galfus-physics-2d
description: >-
  Use this skill to configure 2D rigid bodies, colliders, gravity, impulses,
  and contact events in Realm2D using Rapier 2D via attachPhysics2D.
---

# Galfus Engine 2D Physics (Rapier 2D)

`Realm2D` integrates 2D physics simulation through Rapier 2D, supporting dynamic and fixed rigid bodies, continuous velocity updates, sensor triggers, and 2D spatial queries.

## 1. Initializing 2D Physics

Attach the 2D physics world to your `Realm2D` instance using `attachPhysics2D`:

```typescript
import { attachPhysics2D, createRuntime } from '@galfus/engine';

const runtime = createRuntime(transport);
const realm2d = runtime.createRealm2D();

// Attach physics simulation (default gravity: [0, -9.81])
const physics = await attachPhysics2D(runtime, realm2d, {
  gravity: [0, -9.81]
});
```

## 2. Adding 2D Colliders

Colliders attach to entities with `Model2D` components. Supported shapes: `rectangle`, `circle`, and `polygon`:

```typescript
// Box / Rectangle collider
realm2d.addCollider(boxSprite.entityId, {
  type: 'rectangle',
  halfExtents: [1.0, 1.0] // Half-width and half-height
});

// Circle collider
realm2d.addCollider(ballSprite.entityId, {
  type: 'circle',
  radius: 0.8
});

// Convex polygon collider
realm2d.addCollider(rampSprite.entityId, {
  type: 'polygon',
  vertices: [
    [-1, -0.5],
    [1, -0.5],
    [1, 0.5]
  ]
});
```

## 3. Creating 2D Rigid Bodies

Attach rigid bodies to entities using `physics.addRigidBody`:

```typescript
// Static ground platform
const ground = realm2d.createSprite(
  { dimensions: [20, 1], materialId: groundMat },
  { position: [0, -4] }
);
realm2d.addCollider(ground.entityId, { type: 'rectangle', halfExtents: [10, 0.5] });
physics.addRigidBody(ground.entityId, { type: 'fixed', friction: 0.8 });

// Dynamic character sprite
const player = realm2d.createSprite(
  { dimensions: [1.6, 1.6], materialId: playerMat },
  { position: [0, 2] }
);
realm2d.addCollider(player.entityId, { type: 'circle', radius: 0.8 });
physics.addRigidBody(player.entityId, {
  type: 'dynamic',
  mass: 3.0,
  restitution: 0.2, // Small bounce
  friction: 0.5,
  lockRotations: true // Keep character upright
});
```

## 4. Applying Impulses & Velocity Control

```typescript
// Instant jump impulse [vx, vy]
physics.applyImpulse(player.entityId, [0, 18.0]);

// Horizontal movement
physics.setLinearVelocity(player.entityId, [5.0, currentVy]);

// Torque impulse (angular spin)
physics.applyTorqueImpulse(wheelEntity, 12.0);
```

## 5. Collision Events & Sensor Triggers

Detect when entities collide or pass through sensor zones (pickups, checkpoints):

```typescript
// Sensor collider (doesn't cause physical bounce, triggers event only)
realm2d.addCollider(coinEntity, { type: 'circle', radius: 0.5 }, { isSensor: true });
physics.addRigidBody(coinEntity, { type: 'fixed' });

// Listen to collision events
physics.on('collision_start', (event) => {
  if (event.sensor) {
    console.log(`Entity ${event.entityA} collected sensor ${event.entityB}`);
  }
});
```

## 6. Point Queries (Click to Select)

Test whether a 2D world point intersects any collider:

```typescript
const hits = realm2d.queryPoint([worldX, worldY]);
if (hits.length > 0) {
  console.log(`Clicked on entity: ${hits[0].entityId}`);
}
```

## Best Practices

1. **Lock Rotations for Characters**: Use `{ lockRotations: true }` on platformer characters to prevent physics collisions from tipping them over.
2. **Fixed Platforms**: Mark stationary platforms and walls as `'fixed'` to ensure stable contacts.
3. **Sensor Overlaps**: Set `{ isSensor: true }` in `options` for triggers, coins, and portal checkpoints.

galfus-physics-3d:
---
name: galfus-physics-3d
description: >-
  Use this skill to configure 3D rigid bodies, colliders, gravity, impulses,
  contact events, and raycasting in Realm3D using Rapier 3D via attachPhysics3D.
---

# Galfus Engine 3D Physics (Rapier 3D)

Galfus Engine integrates a high-performance native Rapier 3D physics engine directly into `Realm3D`. Rigid body simulation steps and transform synchronizations happen in the native engine core.

## 1. Initializing 3D Physics

Attach the physics world to your `Realm3D` instance using `attachPhysics3D`:

```typescript
import { attachPhysics3D, createRuntime } from '@galfus/engine';

const runtime = createRuntime(transport);
const realm3d = runtime.createRealm3D();

// Attach physics simulation (default gravity: [0, -9.81, 0])
const physics = await attachPhysics3D(runtime, realm3d, {
  gravity: [0, -9.81, 0]
});
```

## 2. Adding Colliders

Colliders define physical collision boundaries for an entity. Supported shapes include `box`, `sphere`, and `capsule`:

```typescript
// Box collider (halfExtents are half-widths from center)
realm3d.addCollider(crateEntity, {
  type: 'box',
  halfExtents: [0.5, 0.5, 0.5] // 1x1x1 cube
});

// Sphere collider
realm3d.addCollider(ballEntity, {
  type: 'sphere',
  radius: 0.75
});

// Capsule collider
realm3d.addCollider(characterEntity, {
  type: 'capsule',
  halfHeight: 0.9,
  radius: 0.4
});
```

## 3. Creating Rigid Bodies

Add rigid bodies to entities using `physics.addRigidBody`. Supported body types:

- `'dynamic'`: Affected by gravity, forces, impulses, and collisions.
- `'fixed'`: Immovable static level geometry (floors, walls, ramps).
- `'kinematicPosition'` / `'kinematicVelocity'`: Moved programmatically without physical forces pushing back.

```typescript
// Static ground floor
const floor = realm3d.createCube(1, floorMat, {
  position: [0, -0.5, 0],
  scale: [20, 1, 20]
}).entityId;

realm3d.addCollider(floor, { type: 'box', halfExtents: [10, 0.5, 10] });
physics.addRigidBody(floor, { type: 'fixed', friction: 0.7 });

// Dynamic falling crate
const crate = realm3d.createCube(1, crateMat, {
  position: [0, 8, 0],
  scale: [1, 1, 1]
}).entityId;

realm3d.addCollider(crate, { type: 'box', halfExtents: [0.5, 0.5, 0.5] });
physics.addRigidBody(crate, {
  type: 'dynamic',
  mass: 5.0, // Mass in kg
  restitution: 0.4, // Bounciness (0 = no bounce, 1 = perfectly elastic)
  friction: 0.6,
  linearDamping: 0.1,
  angularDamping: 0.2
});
```

## 4. Applying Impulses & Forces

Apply instantaneous impulses (e.g. jumping or explosions) or continuous forces:

```typescript
// Instant jump impulse [x, y, z]
physics.applyImpulse(crate, [0, 25.0, 0]);

// Direct velocity control
physics.setLinearVelocity(crate, [2.0, 0, 0]);

// Continuous wind force applied every tick
runtime.on('tick', () => {
  physics.applyForce(crate, [1.5, 0, 0]);
});
```

## 5. Collision & Sensor Events

Listen for collision events between entities:

```typescript
physics.on('collision_start', (event) => {
  console.log(`Collision started between entity ${event.entityA} and ${event.entityB}`);
});

physics.on('collision_end', (event) => {
  console.log(`Collision ended between entity ${event.entityA} and ${event.entityB}`);
});
```

## 6. Raycasting Queries

Perform line-of-sight and ground checks using `realm3d.raycast`:

```typescript
const origin: [number, number, number] = [0, 2, 0];
const direction: [number, number, number] = [0, -1, 0]; // Downward
const maxDistance = 10.0;

const hit = realm3d.raycast(origin, direction, maxDistance);
if (hit) {
  console.log(`Hit entity ${hit.entityId} at distance ${hit.distance}`);
  console.log(`Contact point:`, hit.point);
  console.log(`Surface normal:`, hit.normal);
}
```

## Best Practices

1. **Entity Order**: Attach colliders to the entity before calling `addRigidBody` so the physics world knows the bounding volume.
2. **Fixed Bodies**: Always designate static obstacles as `'fixed'`. Never use dynamic bodies with infinite mass for static geometry.
3. **Discrete vs CCD**: For fast-moving small projectiles (bullets, fast balls), enable continuous collision detection: `{ ccd: true }`.

galfus-realm-2d:
---
name: galfus-realm-2d
description: >-
  Use this skill to build 2D scenes in Galfus Engine, including 2D orthographic cameras,
  sprites, 2D dynamic lighting, materials, sprite sheets, and 2D entity transforms.
---

# Galfus Engine 2D Realm (Realm2D)

`Realm2D` provides an optimized 2D rendering pipeline featuring batched quad sprites, 2D dynamic point lights, texture atlases, sprite sheets, and orthographic camera views.

## 1. Creating a Realm2D Instance

```typescript
import { createRuntime, type Model2DComponent, SpriteSheet } from '@galfus/engine';

const runtime = createRuntime(transport);
const realm2d = runtime.createRealm2D();
```

## 2. 2D Orthographic Camera

The 2D camera manages view offset and zoom scale:

```typescript
const camera = realm2d.createCamera(
  {
    cameraSlot: 1, // Matches Multiverse Invocation
    orthoScale: 10.0 // Orthographic vertical view height in world units
  },
  {
    position: [0, 0] // Center of the 2D world
  }
);
```

## 3. Materials & Textures

Materials define tint colors and diffuse textures for sprites:

```typescript
// Solid color material
const greenMat = realm2d.createMaterial({
  baseColor: [0.2, 0.8, 0.4, 1.0]
}).materialId;

// Textured material
const textureId = runtime.resources.createTextureFromRgba(width, height, pixelData);
const texturedMat = realm2d.createMaterial({
  baseColor: [1.0, 1.0, 1.0, 1.0],
  textureId
}).materialId;
```

## 4. Creating Sprites

Sprites are 2D textured or colored quads defined by dimensions and world transform:

```typescript
const playerSprite = realm2d.createSprite(
  {
    dimensions: [2.0, 2.0], // Width, Height in world units
    materialId: greenMat
  },
  {
    position: [0, 0],
    rotation: 0, // In radians
    scale: [1, 1]
  }
);
```

## 5. 2D Dynamic Lighting

`Realm2D` includes hardware-accelerated 2D lighting with smooth distance attenuation:

```typescript
const light = realm2d.createLight(
  {
    color: [1.0, 0.85, 0.6], // Warm candlelight
    intensity: 2.5,
    range: 12.0, // Attenuation radius
    lightRadius: 0.5, // Light source core radius
    castShadow: false,
    active: true
  },
  {
    position: [0, 2.0]
  }
);
```

## 6. Sprite Sheets & UV Animation

For pixel art, tilemaps, or animated character frames, use `SpriteSheet` to calculate sub-rectangle UVs:

```typescript
const sheet = new SpriteSheet({
  textureWidth: 256,
  textureHeight: 256,
  frameWidth: 32,
  frameHeight: 32
});

const spriteModel = realm2d.world.getComponent<Model2DComponent>(playerSprite.entityId, 'Model2D');

// Switch to frame 4 (0-indexed)
const uv = sheet.getFrameUv(4);
spriteModel?.setUvOffset(uv.u, uv.v, uv.width, uv.height);
```

## 7. Transforming 2D Entities in the Tick Loop

```typescript
let time = 0;
runtime.on('tick', ({ dt }) => {
  time += dt;

  // Move sprite in a circle
  const x = Math.cos(time * 2.0) * 3.0;
  const y = Math.sin(time * 2.0) * 3.0;

  spriteModel?.setPosition(x, y);
  spriteModel?.setRotation(time); // Rotate continuously
});
```

## Best Practices

- **Pixel Art Alignment**: For pixel-perfect 2D games, set camera zoom and sprite sizes to integer multiples of screen pixels.
- **Batched Rendering**: Sprites sharing the same `materialId` are automatically batched by the native engine for maximum performance.
- **Layering & Depth**: Use the Z component or render order to sort sprites (background, characters, foreground UI).

galfus-realm-2d-helpers:
---
name: galfus-realm-2d-helpers
description: >-
  Use this skill to build user interfaces (UI) and HUDs using @galfus/ui, including buttons,
  panels, dialogs, flex layouts, text fields, and interactive pointer surfaces in 2D realms.
---

# Galfus 2D Helpers & UI System (@galfus/ui)

`@galfus/ui` is the native immediate/retained hybrid UI toolkit for Galfus Engine. It renders high-performance HUDs, game menus, buttons, overlays, and modal dialogs directly inside a `Realm2D` layer.

## 1. Installation

```bash
bun add @galfus/ui
# or
npm install @galfus/ui
```

## 2. Setting Up a UI Surface

A UI system binds to a `Realm2D` instance and a presentation target (window or canvas):

```typescript
import { createRuntime } from '@galfus/engine';
import { createUi, UiButton, UiButtonGroup, UiText } from '@galfus/ui';

const runtime = createRuntime(transport);
const realm2d = runtime.createRealm2D();

const windowId = runtime.platform.window.create({ title: 'Game UI', size: [1280, 720] });
const targetId = runtime.multiverse.createTarget({ kind: { type: 'window', windowId } });

// Create UI system
const ui = createUi(runtime, realm2d, targetId);
const root = ui.root; // Root panel
```

## 3. Buttons & Interactive Controls

Create buttons with hover states, click/activate handlers, and custom variants:

```typescript
// Standard button
const playBtn = new UiButton(root, {
  label: 'Start Game',
  onActivate: () => {
    console.log('Game starting...');
  }
});

// Button group (horizontal or vertical layout)
const group = new UiButtonGroup(root, {
  orientation: 'horizontal',
  spacing: 12
});

const settingsBtn = new UiButton(group.panel, {
  label: 'Settings',
  onActivate: () => openSettings()
});

const exitBtn = new UiButton(group.panel, {
  label: 'Quit',
  onActivate: () => runtime.stop()
});
```

## 4. Text & Labels

Render crisp vector and bitmap text with font family styling:

```typescript
const titleLabel = new UiText({
  text: 'GALFUS ADVENTURE',
  fontSize: 24,
  color: [1.0, 0.9, 0.2, 1.0], // Yellow gold
  x: 20,
  y: 20
});

root.addText(titleLabel);

// Dynamic update in tick loop
titleLabel.setText(`Score: ${currentScore}`);
```

## 5. Panels & Window Overlays

Panels provide backgrounds, borders, 9-slice styling, and nested layout containers:

```typescript
import { Panel, UiDialog } from '@galfus/ui';

// HUD Container Panel (top-right corner)
const hudPanel = new Panel(root, {
  width: 200,
  height: 80,
  backgroundColor: [0.1, 0.12, 0.18, 0.85], // Semi-transparent dark
  borderColor: [0.3, 0.4, 0.6, 1.0],
  borderWidth: 2,
  borderRadius: 6
});

// Modal Dialog
const dialog = new UiDialog(ui, {
  title: 'Game Over',
  modal: true
});

new UiButton(dialog.content, {
  label: 'Retry',
  onActivate: () => {
    dialog.close();
    restartLevel();
  }
});
```

## 6. Layout Anchors & Responsiveness

Use anchors to ensure UI elements scale across different resolutions:

```typescript
// Anchor to top-right
hudPanel.setAnchor({
  horizontal: 'right',
  vertical: 'top',
  offsetX: -20,
  offsetY: 20
});

// Center in screen
dialog.setAnchor({
  horizontal: 'center',
  vertical: 'center'
});
```

## Best Practices

1. **Z-Index Layering**: Render 3D/2D game realms at lower Z-indices (`zIndex: 0`) and the UI invocation at higher Z-indices (`zIndex: 10`) so UI always floats above gameplay.
2. **Pointer Focus**: `@galfus/ui` automatically consumes pointer and keyboard events when hovering over UI controls, preventing clicks from leaking to game objects.
3. **Clean Up**: When closing views or menus, call `.destroy()` or `.close()` on panels and dialogs to free ECS components.

